          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "India's Scientific Contributions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.248,
            0.113,
            0.543,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC, Fig. 8.8), Mumbai, leads advanced neutron-scattering experiments using reactors, such as Dhruva. This has revealed key insights into materials like superconductors, battery electrodes, and drug molecules, helping develop better medicines, energy storage, and industrial alloys right here in India."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.553,
            0.1,
            0.876,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 2,
              "bbox": [
                0.553,
                0.1,
                0.876,
                0.257
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_2.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.561,
                0.26,
                0.853,
                0.275
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.8: Bhabha Atomic Research Centre"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.279,
            0.285,
            0.629,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Research and explore more about Dhruva!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.322,
            0.888,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "By 1869, scientists knew about 69 elements, most of which were found naturally on the Earth. Today, we know about 118 unique chemical elements. Some of these are artificially made, and the search for even more continues."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.376,
            0.888,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, let us explore how elements began to be represented for the sake of simplicity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.423,
            0.54,
            0.445
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8.4 Symbols of Elements"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.448,
            0.889,
            0.516
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "John Dalton realised the need for a standard way to represent elements and compounds to make the study of chemistry easier. In 1803, he introduced the first "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pictorial symbols",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " to represent the known elements. The Fig. 8.9 shows some of the symbols."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.515,
            0.859,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.235,
                0.515,
                0.859,
                0.645
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.376,
                0.648,
                0.738,
                0.663
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.9: Symbols of some elements given by Dalton"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.672,
            0.89,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In 1813, Berzelius suggested that the symbols of elements should be derived from their Latin names. Thus, came alphabetic chemical symbols. Nowadays, the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC), an international scientific organisation, approves the names and symbols of elements. Some of the norms of writing these symbols are:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.233,
            0.76,
            0.89,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Many symbols are the first letter or the first two letters of the name of the element."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.233,
            0.798,
            0.89,
            0.866
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y The first letter of a symbol is always written as a capital letter (uppercase) while the second letter (if there is one) is written as a small letter (lowercase). For example, hydrogen, H; aluminium, Al (not AL); cobalt, Co (not CO), etc."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.869,
            0.89,
            0.92
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y Symbols of some elements are formed from the first letter of the name and a letter other than the second letter in the name. For example, chlorine, Cl; zinc, Zn, etc."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 15,
